  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Pleasant conditions through Sunday morning. \nRain arrives Sunday afternoon and lasts through the night.\n\nPleasant and dry conditions continue through a good portion of \nSunday, with partly cloudy skies and low winds through tonight. \nExpecting to see some radiational cooling tonight with calm \nwinds, but light cloud cover will limit just how far \ntemperatures will drop.... overnight lows likely bottom out in \nlow 40s for most, upper 30s in the higher elevations.\n\nAttention then turns to Sunday afternoon and evening as the \nremnants of Hurricane Isaias make their way north and east with \nthe jet stream. As it exits off the DelMarVa coast and enter our\nwaters south of the Islands, it's expected to strengthen and \npass north of the 40/70 benchmark. PWATs will likely surge to \nnear 2.0\", mostly along the south coast, Cape, and Islands. With\nrain arriving Sunday afternoon (early PM for the interior, \nlater PM for RI and eastern MA) and lingering into Monday, \nrainfall totals will likely be in the 0.75 inch to 1.0 inch \nrange. It's possible some heavier rain bands set up, but \nconfidence is low on where or when exactly those could develop; \ntherefore, some areas could see local higher rainfall totals... \npossibly up to 1.5 inches across the south coast, Cape, and \nIslands. Thankfully, the bulk of the LLJ will be kept aloft and \noffshore, so the worst of the winds will be kept offshore and on\nthe order of 23 to 30 knots.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next quick moving round of rain comes \nSunday afternoon and evening, continuing overnight and exiting \nMonday morning.\n\nLow pressure slowly moves away from southern New England \nMonday. This will gradually bring an end to our showers from \nnorthwest to southeast. Still some disagreement on how quickly \nit all ends. The GFS remains on the more progressive side of the\nsolution envelope. The most likely area for showers to linger \nlonger will be towards the Cape and islands. Further adjustments\nto the timing are possible with later forecasts.\n\nOur region then has a brief drier period Monday night into \nTuesday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Quick round of rain around mid week, then dry \nweather returns by Thursday. \n\nOur region should then get clipped by a pair of low pressures, \neach taking a different track. The first should pass from the \nGreat Lakes across northern New England Tuesday into Tuesday \nnight. The risk for showers arrives across northern MA during \nthe morning, then gradually moves south Tuesday night as the \nassociated cold front approaches. Drying out Wednesday as this \nfront finally moves farther offshore during the day.\n\nHigh pressure over eastern Canada should briefly take over our \nweather Wednesday night into Thursday night.\n\nThe forecast for next Friday and Saturday is uncertain. A low \npressure moving east across Canada could swing its associated \nfronts across our region during this time. The issue continues \nto be one of timing. This low pressure is quite distant from \nsouthern New England. Thinking a slower onset timing is more \nlikely to develop with later forecasts given a high pressure \nover the North Atlantic. Then there is the question of how \nquickly it dries out on Saturday. We should see a period of \nshowers some time Friday into Saturday.",
